# DTF tier manifest: qcmj-webhook-authz  (GHSA-qcmj, 1.6.1 security release)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single (the walking-skeleton topology; the webhook
# authorization defect is storage-independent).
#
# The bug (GHSA-qcmj), two facets:
#   1. Soft management gate. Webhook management verbs (delete / enable / disable
#      / test / rotate-secret / redeliver) authorized on `is_admin OR creator OR
#      repo-accessible`. Creating a webhook is admin-only, so the exploitable
#      path is "repo-accessible": ANY non-admin who merely holds a role on the
#      repository a webhook is attached to can disable/delete/rotate it — a
#      cross-actor BOLA over an egress+signing integration. (A non-admin creator,
#      reachable only via a legacy/seeded row, is the same soft-gate hole.)
#   2. Secret disclosure. rotate-secret returned the raw signing `secret` in its
#      response body.
#
# The fix (1.6.1): management verbs require admin (repo-access + non-admin-creator
# no longer suffice) -> 403; rotate-secret returns only id / secret_digest /
# expiry, never the raw secret.
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (A) BOUNDARY  a repo-accessible non-admin, and a non-admin creator (seeded),
#       calling disable / rotate-secret / delete -> 403, with the DB proving the
#       webhook was NOT mutated (still enabled, digest unchanged, row present).
#       Baseline: 2xx and the webhook IS mutated (RED).
#   (B) CONTROL   an admin disable + rotate on a dedicated webhook -> 2xx (proves
#       management still works; the tier is not a hollow always-403).
#   (C) ONCE-ONLY  since 1.12.0 (artifact-keeper#5235) the admin rotate-secret
#       200 body carries the new raw `secret` once, with Cache-Control: no-store
#       (a receiver cannot verify after the overlap without it; the admin gate in
#       (A) is what keeps it safe). GET, LIST, a repeat rotate (409), a refused
#       non-admin rotate and the backend log must never carry it. On 1.6.1-1.11.x
#       the field was dropped instead; release/1.11.x keeps that assertion.
#
# Webhook signing (and therefore rotate-secret) needs AK_WEBHOOK_SECRET_KEY; the
# shared compose base does not set it, so this oracle injects the key via a
# tier-local compose override and recreates ONLY the backend service on the same
# compose project (labels intact -> the harness `down -v` still reclaims it).
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives an admin login + a user create +
# user login + several webhook creates and back-to-back management probes; the
# base default (ON) would throttle them. A tier that must disable a shipped
# control does so explicitly (design 2.2, same as isolation / ci-token-basic).
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
